Wait, for Static SSR forms the pattern is different. Let me reconsider. The `[SupplyParameterFromForm]` handles it automatically when a POST happens. The form has `method="post"`. I need to detect when the form was submitted. In static SSR, the way to handle this is:

Actually, in Blazor SSR forms, the `[SupplyParameterFromForm]` populates the model on POST. I need to use `OnInitialized` to check if it's a form POST. The standard pattern is:

```razor
@code {
    [SupplyParameterFromForm]
    public EnrollmentRequest Model { get; set; } = new();

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "courseId")]
    public int? PreselectedCourseId { get; set; }

    [CascadingParameter]
    public HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private List<Course> courses = new();
    private bool submitted;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        courses = CourseService.GetAllCourses();

        if (HttpContext != null && HttpMethods.IsPost(HttpContext.Request.Method))
        {
            if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.StudentName) && 
                !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.StudentId) && 
                Model.CourseId > 0)
            {
                CourseService.SubmitEnrollment(Model);
                submitted = true;
            }
        }
        else if (PreselectedCourseId.HasValue)
        {
            Model.CourseId = PreselectedCourseId.Value;
        }
    }
}
```

Use the exact code above for the @code block. The `HttpContext` is available as a `[CascadingParameter]` in static SSR components.

